From lawrence-hunsicker at uiowa.edu  Wed Jan  2 03:16:27 2002
From: lawrence-hunsicker at uiowa.edu (Hunsicker, Lawrence)
Date: Tue, 01 Jan 2002 21:16:27 -0600
Subject: S-Plus approach to SAS first and last operators
Message-ID: <1508FB10AFDBD211854D0090273C410708A1F6AE@com83-178.medicine.uiowa.edu>

Greetings:

I am a SAS programmer now trying to get up and running in S-Plus.  Of course
the programming style is quite different.  I have figured out a lot of ways
to translate into S-Plus what I used to do in SAS, but I am stuck on how to
do what I used to do in SAS by using the first. and last. operators.  

Specifically, when I am processing a file in which there may be multiple
records per subject, and I have to initialize at the beginning of a set of
records on a new subject, or wind something up at the end of such a block, I
have been used to use the following sort of SAS commands:

        data such-and-so;
        set a-source-file;
        by id;
        if first.id then do-something;  /*  initialize  */
        ...
        steps for all records;
        ...
        if last.id then do-something-else;  /*  wrap up  */

I have been able to duplicate this in S-Plusd with sort of a kludge by
setting a variable (not in either the input or output dataset) to null at
the beginning of the processing, testing whether the id equals this variable
each time that I handle a new record, and resetting the external variable as
part of the initializing for a new id.  I have kludged the wrap up after
reading in a variable by querying whether there is a next record, and if so,
whether its id is still unchanged.  (I wrap up if either there is no next
record, or, if there is, if the id in that record is different.)

This seems to work OK.  But S-Plus emphasizes that we should be able to
handle most operations using vector processing.  Of course to do the above I
have to use a do loop, stepping through the records.  This seems pretty
inefficient.

Is there a better way to handle this sort of thing?

Many thanks in advance to any who can help me on this.

Larry Hunsicker

