From bb2 at duke.edu  Fri Jan  4 18:50:32 2002
From: bb2 at duke.edu (Brian Beckage)
Date: Fri, 04 Jan 2002 13:50:32 -0500
Subject: Summary: Matrix question
Message-ID: <3.0.5.32.20020104135032.00fef780@mail-bb.acpub.duke.edu>

Thanks to everyone who responded to my original question (copied below).
I've summarized the responses below in no particular order.

Brian

>If, for example, I have a 3 by 3 matrix called 'tmp' that contains 1:9,
is there a simple way (that avoids a 'for' loop) of retrieving the row and
column number of a given entry?  
>
>Something of the form Function(tmp,9) that returns [3,3]?  
>
>     [,1] [,2] [,3] 
>[1,]    1    4    7
>[2,]    2    5    8
>[3,]    3    6    9

Several replies suggested something along the lines of

        which(tmp==9,arr.ind=TRUE)

but this did not work in Splus 2000 R3 for Windows.  However, which() could
be used after first vectorizing the matrix as suggested (and implemented)
by several folks.

##### From Jason Connor

Function <- function(mat,value)  { 
xy <- cbind(rep(1:dim(mat)[1],dim(mat)[2]),sort(rep(1:dim(mat)[2],
dim(mat)[1])))  
xy[c(mat==value),] 
}

##### From Iyue Sung 

tmprow<-row(tmp)
tmpcol<-col(tmp)
tmprow[tmp==9] 
tmpcol[tmp==9]

##### Bill Dunlap also made suggestions along these lines and illustrated
how to expand it for arrays...

You can use row(tmp) or col(tmp).  For arrays of any number of dimensions
you can use slice.index(tmp,dim.number): slice.index(tmp,1) is equivalent
to row(tmp) and slice.index(tmp,2) is col(tmp).

##### From Stephen Kaluzny
"ijval"<-
function(x, val)
{
        cbind(row(x)[x == val], col(x)[x == val])
}

##### From Bert Gunter

if (nr,nc)=dim(tmp)  then

cbind(rep(1:nr,nc),rep(1:nc,e=nr))[tmp==9,]

does what you want. This works because matrices are actually vectors stored
by default in column major order and the expression in the brackets is
silently coerced to a vector because that is what it must be as a row index.

##### Here were some functions that Brad Biggerstaff received from a
posting several years ago:

index<-function(element, object, in.object = T)
{
        # return indices of elements not in object
        if(in.object) # return indices of elements in object
                seq(along = object)[!is.na(match(object, element))] else
seq(along = object)[is.na(match(object, element))]
}

mat.index<- function(e,x){
        rownum <- index(e,x) %% nrow(x)
        if(rownum==0) rownum <- nrow(x)
        colnum <- index(e,x[rownum,])
        c(rownum,colnum)
}

###### From Steven C. Wofsy
index.f<-function(tmp,MyValue){
element.number <-  (1:length(tmp))[as.numeric(tmp)==MyValue]
col.number<-trunc(element.number/nrow(tmp)) + 1
row.number<-element.number-(col.number -1 )*nrow(tmp)
c(row.number,col.number)
}

