From phillip.stafford at motorola.com  Mon Jan  7 20:12:11 2002
From: phillip.stafford at motorola.com (Stafford Phillip-APS095)
Date: Mon, 07 Jan 2002 14:12:11 -0600
Subject: Memory issue resolved
Message-ID: <07077F6C2A38D511920800D0B7821B3204022B59@il06exm01.corp.mot.com>

Thanks to those who responded about my memory problem.  I would thank them but 
I forgot their names.
The problem, in case anyone else has this issue, is the method by which Windows 
manages memory leaks and memory allocation tables.  Once memory in Windows is 
fragmented beyond a threshold value, it should automatically issue a cleanup - 
cache, virtual, and physical, which is why occassionally your hard drive spins 
without reason.  The problem I had was before S+ could issue an internal 
cleanup, Windows allocation tables were corrupted and thus the blue screen.  
This will occassionally cause file allocation table corruption, which accounts 
for the S+ command file problem.  With NT you could be strict about physical 
memory banks but 2000 mixes the physical memory banks, a warning to those with 
sketchy memory.

Neither UNIX not Linus has this problem so if there is enough physical and swap 
memory in the machine there won't be a problem.

I still hold that S+ can operate reliably on files hundreds of megs in size.

Phillip Stafford
Motorola Life Sciences

