From P.J.Wells at open.ac.uk  Mon Jan 14 15:08:28 2002
From: P.J.Wells at open.ac.uk
Date: Mon, 14 Jan 2002 15:08:28 +0000
Subject: Legends in pie charts
Message-ID: <F9D44AC0D89CD311B9E3000629385C40044D9E0F@marconi.open.ac.uk>

OK -- I've been through the archives and read the controversy over whether
or not these are a Good Thing.

Regardless of the ultimate answer to this (presumably 42 :-), but see
below), I have to produce one and possibly several.

Moreover, it would be helpful if the chart(s) could include a legend, rather
than relying on labels to identify the slices.

I produced a trial version using the GUI (trial, because I was testing the
code to calculate what I am charting: again, see below) and, all
unwittingly, invited the GUI to add the desired legend -- a process which
leads to the following points:

1) It seems that MathSoft may not have intended Insert>Legend... to have any
effect on pie charts, since

        (a) the legend is added before the implied Legend dialogue box
appears

        (b) a legend entry is generated for *each data item*, rather than
each slice

        (c) producing the legend is very time-consuming even for data with
n=1000

However, selecting the surplus legend items and deleting them produces the
desired result.

2) Investigating the structure of the resulting object in the Object
Explorer reveals the following features, which strike this ignorant observer
as curious:

        (a) one branch of GraphSheet is GraphSheet>TextGraph>PiePlot, while
the other is GraphSheet>Page1>TextGraph>PiePlot.
                
        (b) both versions of PiePlot contain Slice1:n, where n is
length(data) -- and as far as I can tell, the two versions are identical,
looking at their properties.
Nonetheless, the pie slices drawn appear to reflect the data correctly, in
the sense that the slices are the appropriate proportions of the whole

        (c) the properties of the "slices" don't appear to bear any obvious
relation to the chart displayed (such as the colour coinciding with the
slice in which the data item should appear)

        (d) adding a legend causes a Legend object to appear beside both the
PiePlot objects.

Can anyone explain what is happening (and if possible suggest how I might
get legends in finite time, since my real data has length(x) = 10^5)?

3) Why pie charts? Well, I see the force of the point that it's difficult to
distinguish small differences. But what if the *point* of the chart is to
demonstrate that the relative shares essentially the same?

Suppose, for example, that one is investigating the proportion of national
research spending devoted to defence, health and education (and a list of
other things).

If it turns out that defence R&D is approximately equal to the sum of health
and education R&D one has an important fact which a pie chart shows in a way
that anyone can understand.

In this context, worrying about whether the chart shows clearly that (say)
health R&D is 10 per cent bigger than education R&D seems besides the point.

My case is rather different to this. My project is ultimately about the
ratio of two variables (company net income and company capital, since you
ask -- i.e. I am looking at profit rates).

Counter-intuitively, the sign of the capital measure can be negative (in the
case where capital is defined to be net assets, including financial assets).

Evidently, one would like to have some idea of the relative proportion of
cases where the profit rate is positive because the firm's net income is
positive, and those where although net income is negative the profit is
positive because the net assets are negative (and similarly for negative
profit rates).

A pie chart showing the proportion of cases falling in the four quadrants s+
k+, s- k+, s+ k-, and s- k- seems an obvious way to approach this.

