From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Tue Jan 15 08:06:29 2002
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Tue, 15 Jan 2002 08:06:29 +0000
Subject: Re: arima.mle() and AIC
In-Reply-To: <3.0.5.32.20020114171448.01062080@mail-bb.acpub.duke.edu>
References: <3.0.5.32.20020114171448.01062080@mail-bb.acpub.duke.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0201150802310.14906-100000@gannet.stats>

On Mon, 14 Jan 2002, Brian Beckage wrote:

> Hi,
>
> Should centering a covariate used in an ARMA model change the value of AIC
> or the AR or MA coefficients?  Below, I fit an AR(1) model to the same data
> but using a centered (mean subtracted from each value) or uncentered
> covariate.  Both the AIC and value of the AR coefficient change depending
> on whether I have centered the covariate.  I get similar results when
> fitting ARMA(p,q) models.  Is this as it should be or is there some other
> problem?

Yes it should.  arima.mle does not include an overall level (mean) in the
model. Although current S-PLUS reports an AIC value, that is incorrect as
arima.mle does not do maximum-likelihood fitting (it fits a conditional
likelihood to some of the data).

Both are traps that catch most users from time to time. For the first, add
a constant column to your xreg matrix.

