I suspect it is a scoping problem (the value of a variable is
different from what you think it should be) but I'm kind of tired
right now and I can see where it would be.

I can offer two suggestions:
 - add trace = TRUE to the call to nls so you can see the progress of
   the iterations.
 - once you figure out why nls isn't working inside your function,
   you can reduce the dimensionality of the nls problem by using the
   "plinear" algorithm.  Try

nls(logload ~ b0 + b1 * logflow + b2 * (logflow - Bp)
  *(pnorm(logflow-Bp,0,0.01)), data= yellbk.dat,
  start=c(Bp=0, b0=7.229839, b1 = .9938712, b2 =0.6397375), trace = TRUE)

versus

nls(logload ~ cbind(1,logflow,(logflow - Bp)*(pnorm(logflow-Bp,0,0.01))),
    data= yellbk.dat, start=c(Bp=0), alg = "plinear", trace = TRUE)

to see the difference.

Notice that your function could then be written

 sel.breakpoint <- function(x, y, nbreak=1, plotit=T) {
   if(nbreak == 1) { # single breakpoint
     nls.out <- nls(Y ~ cbind(1, X, (X - Bp)*(pnorm(X-Bp,0,0.01))),
                    data=cbind(Y=y,X=x),start=c(Bp=median(x)),
                    alg="plinear")
     print(summary(nls.out)$parameters)
     if(plotit)
       plot(nls.out)
     return(nls.out$parameters[1])
   }
 }

By the way, if your problem is a scoping problem, your function will
probably run the way you intend in R because R's scoping rules are
different from those of S-PLUS.

