Subject: Use of offset() in formulas
Message-ID: <200201180329.g0I3ToI04402@biomat1.marc.hort.cri.nz>

In the White book, P 222, the specified way of using offset is 
given in the form:

y ~ dose + age + offset(prior.fit)

I was comparing GLMs in Splus and R and noticed the following:

In the R dialect, offset is given as an argument to glm, so that one
could specify in a call to glm:

formula = y ~ dose + age + offset(prior.fit)

OR
 
formula = y ~ dose + age, offset = prior.fit

and get identical results.

However, in Splus 3.4, the first form gives what R does, but the
second form give results, though not very different, not very close,
particularly the intercept.

There does not appear to be much documentation explaining what is
going on.  There is no mention in the Splus documentation to indicate
that using offset as an argument to glm would work at all, yet
evidently it is designed to do something.

My question is what is it doing?  (I could give an example if it were
necessary.)

