From Stephanie.Mahevas at ifremer.fr  Fri Jan 18 07:54:55 2002
From: Stephanie.Mahevas at ifremer.fr (Stephanie MAHEVAS)
Date: Fri, 18 Jan 2002 08:54:55 +0100
Subject: ACF for gls object
In-Reply-To: <3C3DC598.CE23DFCB@ifremer.fr>
Message-ID: <3C47D4CF.7EE837D3@ifremer.fr>

Thanks to D. McKenzie, Renaud Lancelot, C. Kruitwagen and Jose Pinheiro
for their constructive answers that I summarized below.

Stephanie MAHEVAS a écrit :
> 
> Dear s+users
> 
> we have some problems concerning ACF function applied to a gls object.
> When looking at the data, we detected an autocorrelation of order 1
> using acf function. Then we fitted two gls models : one with
> autocorrelated error corAR1 and one without any autocorrelation. We used
> ACF on the residuals of both models and obtained identical plots.
> However, the anova function on the two models indicated that the first
> model was better according to the LogLikelihood ratio test.
> Has anybody encountered the same problem and found a solution?
> thanks in advance.
> 

Renaud Lancelot a écrit :
> 
> Bonjour Stéphanie,
> 
> Les deux figures sont-elles vraiment identiques (échelle, etc.) ? De
> toute manière, c'est rare d'arriver à retirer toute l'autocorrélation
> (certains disent même que c'est impossible: cf JL Foulley, INRA
> biométrie). Le principal est d'en tenir compte, les méthodes étant
> souvent suffisamment robustes pour s'accomoder d'une faible
> autocorrélation "résiduelle".
> 
> Cordialement,
> 
> Renaud
> 

"C. Kruitwagen" a écrit :
> 
> Dear Stephanie,
> 
> I suspect your problem might have to do with the fact that the default ACF
> plot generated by the GUI of S-PLUS uses standardized residuals. By using
> the Command Interface you can obtain an ACF plot of the normalized
> residuals. I think you will be able to see the difference then. For details
> I refer you to Ch 5.3 of Mixed-Effects Models in S and S-PLUS by Pinheiro
> and Bates.
> 
> Regards,
> Cas.

jose.pinheiro at pharma.novartis.com a écrit :
> 
> Dear Stephanie,
> 
> > we have some problems concerning ACF function applied to a gls object.
> > When looking at the data, we detected an autocorrelation of order 1
> > using acf function. Then we fitted two gls models : one with
> > autocorrelated error corAR1 and one without any autocorrelation. We used
> > ACF on the residuals of both models and obtained identical plots.
> > However, the anova function on the two models indicated that the first
> > model was better according to the LogLikelihood ratio test.
> > Has anybody encountered the same problem and found a solution?
> 
> The use of an autocorrelation function in the gls model is not the same as
> transforming the data, so the pattern should not disappear in the
> standardized or raw residuals. That is, you are simply taking into account
> in the model that the autocorrelation is present. In order to investigate
> the success of the model, you may look at the normalized residuals
> (described on page 239 of our book, Mixed-Effects Models in S and S-PLUS
> and used in the subsequent example).
> 
> Hope this helps,
> --José
