From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Jan 18 07:57:36 2002
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Fri, 18 Jan 2002 07:57:36 +0000
Subject: Re: Use of offset() in formulas
In-Reply-To: <200201180329.g0I3ToI04402@biomat1.marc.hort.cri.nz>
References: <200201180329.g0I3ToI04402@biomat1.marc.hort.cri.nz>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0201180748020.24271-100000@gannet.stats>

On Fri, 18 Jan 2002, Patrick Connolly wrote:

> In the White book, P 222, the specified way of using offset is
> given in the form:
>
> y ~ dose + age + offset(prior.fit)
>
> I was comparing GLMs in Splus and R and noticed the following:
>
> In the R dialect, offset is given as an argument to glm, so that one
> could specify in a call to glm:
>
> formula = y ~ dose + age + offset(prior.fit)
>
> OR
>
> formula = y ~ dose + age, offset = prior.fit
>
> and get identical results.
>
> However, in Splus 3.4, the first form gives what R does, but the
> second form give results, though not very different, not very close,
> particularly the intercept.
>
> There does not appear to be much documentation explaining what is
> going on.  There is no mention in the Splus documentation to indicate
> that using offset as an argument to glm would work at all, yet
> evidently it is designed to do something.
>
> My question is what is it doing?  (I could give an example if it were
> necessary.)

Nothing.  Example from V&R (using S-PLUS 3.4, which I seem still to have
installed but never use these days):

options(contrasts = c("contr.treatment", "contr.poly"))
ldose <- rep(0:5, 2)
numdead <- c(1, 4, 9, 13, 18, 20, 0, 2, 6, 10, 12, 16)
sex <- factor(rep(c("M", "F"), c(6, 6)))
SF <- cbind(numdead, numalive = 20 - numdead)
budworm.lg <- glm(SF ~ sex*ldose, family = binomial)
summary(budworm.lg, cor = F)
Coefficients:
                 Value Std. Error    t value
(Intercept) -2.9935414  0.5525295 -5.4178852
        sex  0.1749865  0.7781556  0.2248733
      ldose  0.9060363  0.1670577  5.4234939
  sex:ldose  0.3529131  0.2699444  1.3073547

> budworm.lg <- glm(SF ~ sex*ldose, family = binomial, offset = 20)
> summary(budworm.lg, cor = F)
Coefficients:
                 Value Std. Error    t value
(Intercept) -2.9935414  0.5525295 -5.4178852
        sex  0.1749865  0.7781556  0.2248733
      ldose  0.9060363  0.1670577  5.4234939
  sex:ldose  0.3529131  0.2699444  1.3073547

As with many S functions, extra arguments are accepted and passed around
(in glm to glm.control) but ignored if not relevant.  So it is correct
that there be no documentation: in any case the ultimate documentation is
the code.

As I understand it, the use of offset as an argument in glm in R is
historical, before it was implemented as part of the formula-handling.
glm under R is different in quite a few ways from glm under S, BTW.

Another trap is that S accepts an offset in a formula for lm and ignores
it.

