From therneau at mayo.edu  Fri Jan 18 17:00:30 2002
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Terry Therneau)
Date: Fri, 18 Jan 2002 11:00:30 -0600
Subject: Re: Frailty Cox model
In-Reply-To: <83410141993.20020118113246@vet-alfort.fr>
Message-ID: <200201181700.LAA17000@rocky.mayo.edu>

   It appears that the book isn't as clear as it could be...
   
   The EM algorithm, for Cox/gamma frailty models, is unbelievably slow to
converge.  The book shows that for theta=variance of the frailty = some
FIXED value, that the EM is equivalent to a particular penalized likelihood --
the latter is fast.
   The Splus code is organized thus:
        a. Pick a value for theta
        b. Do standard Newton-Raphson interation, on the penalized problem,
                to find the solution to all the other parameters (betas).
        c. Using the results of b, compute the marginal likelihood; that is,
        the likelihood for this value of theta, but with theta viewed as a
        free parameter rather than as one fixed in advance.  (Some of the
        ancillary computations resulting from step b make this easy to do).
        Then compute a new (better) guess for theta.
           If we haven't converged (new theta almost the same as current one),
        return to step a for another iteration.
            
The a/c loop is labeled "outer" iterations in the printout, the work done in
b is "inner" iterations.  The marginal likelihood is currently labeled
"EM likelihood", but that label is changing to the more appropriate "marginal
likelihood" whenever my latest changes propogate out into the commercial
product.  The final likelihood from step b is also printed, i.e., the one
treating theta as a pre-specified fixed parameter.  The likelihood values
from viewpoint b and viewpoint c happen to agree at theta=0, so only one
NULL likelihood is printed.         

  I hope this helps.
  
        Terry Therneau
        

