From haibotang at yahoo.com  Fri Jan 18 18:49:52 2002
From: haibotang at yahoo.com (haibo tang)
Date: Fri, 18 Jan 2002 10:49:52 -0800
Subject: Summary: building block-diag matrices
Message-ID: <20020118184952.46729.qmail@web13303.mail.yahoo.com>

Hi,

I thank all who have replied to my question. I am
really impressed by you guys' quick replies. All the
answers I have received work for the particular
example. Scott Chasalow and Julian Taylor provide the
same general solution to the question and that's
exactly the very problem I have in mind. So I will
post here only Scott's version, for its better
documentation. Julian has the same function.

"bdiag"<-
function(x)
{
#  DATE WRITTEN:  2 December 1995             LAST
REVISED:  3 December 
1995
#  AUTHOR:  Scott Chasalow (sasssc at scri.sari.ac.uk)
#  CURRENT ADDRESS (from 1 Feb 1999):
scott.chasalow at cereon.com
#
#  x: a list of matrices (any non-matrix component is
coerced to a 
matrix
via 
#     a call to as.matrix();  in particular,  a vector
becomes a column
matrix
)
#
# VALUE:  a matrix having the components of x as
blocks on the 
diagonal, 
#         and zero elements elsewhere.
#
# EXAMPLE:  bdiag(list(1, matrix(2:7,2,3),8:10))
#
# SEE ALSO:  kronecker
#
     if(!is.list(x)) stop("x not a list")
     n <- length(x)
     if(n == 0)
          return(NULL)
     x <- lapply(x, function(y)
     if(length(y)) as.matrix(y) else stop("Zero-length
component in 
x"))
     d <- array(unlist(lapply(x, dim)), c(2, n))
     rr <- d[1,  ]
     cc <- d[2,  ]
     rsum <- sum(rr)
     csum <- sum(cc)
     out <- array(0, c(rsum, csum))
     ind <- array(0, c(4, n))
     rcum <- cumsum(rr)
     ccum <- cumsum(cc)
     ind[1, -1] <- rcum[ - n]
     ind[2,  ] <- rcum
     ind[3, -1] <- ccum[ - n]
     ind[4,  ] <- ccum
     imat <- array(1:(rsum * csum), c(rsum, csum))
     iuse <- apply(ind, 2, function(y, imat)
     {
          imat[(y[1] + 1):y[2], (y[3] + 1):y[4]]
     }
     , imat = imat)
     iuse <- as.vector(unlist(iuse))
     out[iuse] <- unlist(x)
     out
}

Best,

Haibo

