From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Tue Jan 22 07:50:48 2002
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Tue, 22 Jan 2002 07:50:48 +0000
Subject: Re: Scoping Rules
In-Reply-To: <85256B48.0070647E.00@smta00.hc-sc.gc.ca>
References: <85256B48.0070647E.00@smta00.hc-sc.gc.ca>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0201220745230.640-100000@gannet.stats>

On Mon, 21 Jan 2002 Peter_Moore at hc-sc.gc.ca wrote:

> I am running S-Plus 2000 on Win 95.
> I am writing a function and have run into a problem which I think pertains to
> scoping rules.
> In my function I define a vector called r.s.  I use the variable in a linear
> model as follows:
>
> ms <- lm(rs ~ ds)
>
> where ds is another vector.  When I make a call to predict() as shown below:
>
> fs <- predict(ms, se.fit = T)
>
> I am given an error message: "Error: object rs not found"  I have tried using
>    assign() and get()
> within the call to predict(), but the same error message results.

If this is inside a function, you need to assign the variable (is it
`rs' or `r.s.'?) in frame 1.

Example:

myfit <- function()
{
   rs <- rnorm(10)
   ds <- runif(10)
   assign("rs", rs, f = 1)
   assign("ds", ds, f = 1)
   fit <- lm(rs ~ ds)
   predict(fit, se.fit = T)
}
myfit()

fails without both assign lines.

