independent
>variable x. In order to make the problem tractable, a Fourier
transform
>of the complex signal is peformed and the complex signal is then
>characterized by the median frequency of the Fourier transform.

** Don't you mean median power over the frequencies? 

>The relation between signal and independent variable x is then
characterized
>by a regression analysis. My question is as follows: Is it proper to
>explore the relation using a regression given the distributional
>difficulty expressing the sample distribution of a median? 

Sure! However, it may not give you the information you're looking for,
as,
e.g., power at a set of frequencies may be of interest. In general,
the
question you are asking (I think) is: How does my signal (an
essentially
continuous, periodic ?, curve) depend on explanatory covariates.
Clearly,
not only the amplitude, but the phase, shape (i.e., the whole spectrum)
etc.
may change in response to the covariates.

