You could look in the on-line Complements for `S Programming', which have a
lot of examples. (Start at http://www.stats.ox.ac.uk/pub/MASS3/Sprog.)
However, using Fortran is tricky.  I don't know the compiler you mention
and AFAIK the only supported compilers are Compaq Visual Fortran (which is
what chapter -m supports) and the mingw version of g77.

There are all sorts of traps, but if your Fortran code makes no calls to
functions in the sqpe.dll and does not use character variables, I think all
you have to do is to get your compiler to make a DLL called S.dll which
exports the entry points you want to call.  Depending on the compiler, you
many need to use .C not .Fortran. If you can manage to create S.dll and
tell me the output of pedump -e S.dll I can advise further offline.

chapter.exe was written by Insightful, I believe by Rich Gates.  There is
on-line help for it, not too accurate ....

