Subject: Re: survival analysis using SPLUS
Message-ID: <OFFBBFEE22.42BCE67F-ON85256B50.006B24EE@hivresearch.org>

SPLUS USERS:

I am working on a survival analysis using SPLUS and having difficultly
fully understanding the use of the contrast function to assign correct
contrast to my variables of interest.  For example, I have 3 treatment
groups (1 placebo and 2 active) and several other key variables that I have
put into a semi-parametric cox model, which looks something like this:

cert.cox1<-coxph(Surv(ELAP.DAY, TERM==1)~AC+CDC.STG+GENDER+RACE,
data=BET2A)

where ELAP.DAY is the vector of survival times or times to censor, TERM is
an indicator value of 0 for terminated, 1 for non-terminated, AC is a
categorical variable where 0=placebo, 1=treatment 1, 2=treatment 2, CDC.STG
is also categorical with 0=A, 1=B, 2=C.

When I run the cox model, the output I get looks like:

> cert.cox1<-coxph(Surv(ELAP.DAY, TERM==1)~AC+CDC.STG+GENDER+RACE,
data=BET2A)
> cert.cox1
Call:
coxph(formula = Surv(ELAP.DAY, TERM == 1) ~ AC + CDC.STG + GENDER + RACE,
data = BET2A)

            coef exp(coef) se(coef)      z        p
      AC -0.0242     0.976   0.0823 -0.294 0.770000
CDC.STG1  0.3727     1.452   0.0870  4.283 0.000018
CDC.STG2  0.0344     1.035   0.0564  0.611 0.540000
  GENDER  0.0807     1.084   0.1014  0.796 0.430000
   RACE1  0.1551     1.168   0.2321  0.668 0.500000
   RACE2 -0.1764     0.838   0.0853 -2.068 0.039000
   RACE3 -0.1093     0.896   0.0701 -1.560 0.120000
   RACE4  0.1343     1.144   0.1443  0.930 0.350000

Likelihood ratio test=25.9  on 8 df, p=0.00111  n= 450

The problem I see is that SPLUS has factored CDC.STG and RACE but not AC
and GENDER, i.e the p-value I get for AC is the overall P-value for that
variable, whereas I am getting n-1 p-values for the factored variables
CDC.STG and RACE.

How should this look?  When I imported the data into SPLUS, I had already
assigned catergorical values to these variables, which I think is causing
SPLUS to think of them as numbers and is assigning weights.  Should I be
putting a contrasts command into my regression model?

Thank you in advance,

Regards;

LaRee

From ltracy at hivresearch.org  Tue Jan 29 20:11:59 2002
From: ltracy at hivresearch.org
Date: Tue, 29 Jan 2002 15:11:59 -0500
Subject: Re: survival analysis using SPLUS
In-Reply-To: <OFFBBFEE22.42BCE67F-ON85256B50.006B24EE@hivresearch.org>
Message-ID: <OF625A7458.3B10A55C-ON85256B50.006EA6A5@hivresearch.org>

Thanks to Jan Brogger, Andy Liaw, and Jason Conner for their rapid
response. They all suggested changing my options for contrasts from the
helmert method to the following:

options(contrasts=c("contr.treatment", "contr.poly"))

then fit the survial analysis again.

The S-PLUS default is Helmert contrasts which are harder to interpret.

Many Thanks!

LaRee

